# std/ci-status (design/stack-publish.md, "ci-status"): an entry for an
# OPERATION OF THE STEP. It reads GitHub and writes nothing, but `@op` keeps it
# in llm-step, which already holds the github-token reader: a worker of its own
# would need its own secret grant, and stack-merge-to-master reads the same thing before
# it merges. This entry exists to give the operation a path, a help and a place
# in `tool_help`/`run_tool`. The image it curries is never run.
HELP=<<END
What CI says about a branch on GitHub. The first line is the verdict: passing, pending (something has not finished), FAILING (something failed; neutral and skipped checks pass), or no checks. Then every check run and commit status on its head, one per line with its state and link, the failing ones first. With wait-min it waits, reading again every 15 seconds, until something fails or nothing is pending, for at most that many minutes; use start and result to wait in the background. The branch can be a branch name, a commit hash, or, with stack, a layer's name (02-tests) or its name without the number, which means that layer's branch (with branch-prefix in front). The repository comes from stack's 00-base.source.json unless given.
@param branch The branch, commit, or layer to read.
@param [stack] A stack directory, such as sources/repo: names the repository, and lets branch be a layer.
@param [repository] HTTPS repository URL on github.com, without credentials. Required without stack.
@param [branch-prefix] Text put before a layer's branch name, the same value given to stack-publish.
@param [wait-min] Minutes to wait for CI to settle: it returns as soon as a check fails or none is pending, and after that long says it is still pending. Default 0, read once.
@op ci-status
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/llm-step --help=$HELP
